      <h2 id="why">Why read this</h2>
      <p class="lead">If you have ever stared at a BaZi chart or an I Ching reading and thought "I have no idea what I'm looking at" - this lesson is the antidote. Yin and Yang is the single idea underneath every school of Chinese metaphysics, and it is much simpler than most explanations make it.</p>

      <h2 id="what">What Yin and Yang are</h2>
      <p>Yin and Yang are not good and evil, not male and female, not a duality to pick a side of. They are two complementary forces that describe how anything moves, changes, and balances.</p>
      <p><strong>Yang</strong> is the outward, active, bright, rising side. Day is yang relative to night. <strong>Yin</strong> is the inward, receptive, still, gathering side. Night is yin relative to day. Notice: neither is better - each is simply the other's partner in a cycle.</p>

      <h2 id="analogy">A daily-life analogy</h2>
      <div class="analogy"><b>Everyday analogy.</b> Think of a breath. Inhale - expanding, taking in, yang. Exhale - releasing, settling, yin. You never hold only one. A life is a series of breaths, and Chinese metaphysics reads patterns the same way - as cycles of yin and yang, not as fixed states.</div>

      <h2 id="example">A 30-second example</h2>
      <p>The Zhou Yi's very first line of commentary says: <em>"One yin, one yang - this is called the Way."</em> It is not a definition you memorize; it is a rhythm you notice. A chart, a year, a personality - each is some particular balance of the two forces at a given moment.</p>

      <h2 id="where">Where this leads</h2>
      <p>The next lesson adds the Five Elements - the five ways that balance can be made of. Then the Zodiac gives each a face, and BaZi shows how the four pillars of your birth moment hold all of it together. One step at a time, no jargon required.</p>

